# R42 revision 9 — check 16, second leg. The first leg (evidence/binstall-signing.log)
# read five files and established that a minisign signature is checked only where the
# crate publishes a key. This leg asks the complementary question over the WHOLE source
# tree at the immutable tag v1.23.0, rather than over the files that leg happened to open:
# is a signature the only thing cargo-binstall can verify downloaded bytes against, or does
# a checksum path exist somewhere too?
#
# WHY THIS LEG WAS REWRITTEN FOR REVISION 9. Revision 8's payload asserted only the tree
# hash; its purported positive control, its negative control and its no-digest sweep merely
# printed their status, so none of them could fail the script. That was the round-8
# rejection (finding F1). Every claimed control is now an ASSERTION: it prints
# `ASSERT-OK <name>` or `ASSERT-FAIL <name>`, and a failure makes the script exit 1. The
# script also asserts its own assertion COUNT (23), so a silently skipped assertion fails
# the run.
#
# THE TREE CONTROL WAS STRENGTHENED, AND ONE REVISION-8 CLAIM ABOUT IT WAS WRONG. Revision 8
# hashed ONE file, `crates/bin/src/args.rs`. Demonstrating that control showed it does not
# separate v1.23.0 from the adjacent v1.22.0 — every file either leg of check 16 reads is
# byte-identical at those two tags, which section B0 of
# `evidence/binstall-assertion-controls.log` asserts rather than claims (8 of 8 identical).
# Two things follow and both are recorded rather than glossed. First, the control
# authenticates the CONTENT every finding rests on, not the release number; where the
# content is identical no finding of check 16 would differ, so that is not a gap. Second,
# one file is still a weaker control than the eight files the findings actually rest on, so
# this leg now hashes all eight: the five the first leg fetches at the same tag and the
# three this leg reads. The perturbation used to demonstrate it is tag v1.21.0, whose
# `args.rs` genuinely differs (`2e77dce1…`), and the perturbed run fails as required.
#
# THE OTHER CONTROLS ARE DEMONSTRATED TOO. `PERTURB=<name>` substitutes exactly ONE input.
# The third leg runs this script once per control and requires the run to exit non-zero AND
# to print that control's own `ASSERT-FAIL` line: `tree` (v1.21.0), `dvcount` (enumerate
# `DataVerifier` over one sub-crate instead of the tree), `dupsite` (a second
# `Sha256Digest::new` call site), `poscntl` (point the positive control at a file that does
# not carry its pattern), `blake3` (write the negative control's own pattern into the tree)
# and `sweep` (sweep the registry crate, which does compare a digest, instead of the
# fetchers). The two perturbations that edit the unpacked tree restore it, and the third leg
# asserts the restoration by rerunning this script clean afterwards and requiring an output
# identical to the first clean run.
#
# Runs: 2026-09-14T23:01:55Z and 23:01:56Z, which is 2026-09-14 16:01 America/Los_Angeles.
# The script prints no timestamp of its own, so these stamps are the outputs' mtimes. The
# execution recorded below is the FIRST of the two.
#
# Working directory: the run directory. Scratch directory: <scratch>/r42r9/ctl/leg2, outside
# the repository. This leg installs nothing and runs no third-party binary: it fetches an
# archive, unpacks it and greps it. It reads no file inside the repository and writes none,
# so committing this revision cannot change what it does.
#
# RERUN RULE.
# (a) On THIS host: byte-for-byte identical. Two consecutive clean runs produced a zero-line
#     diff, which section A of `evidence/binstall-assertion-controls.log` asserts. There are
#     no timings in this leg and no absolute path is printed.
# (b) On ANY OTHER host: identical as well, including the final line
#     `CHECK16B RESULT: PASSED`. The tag is immutable and nothing here reads host state, a
#     target triple, $CARGO_HOME or the network beyond the one archive.
#
# WHAT THIS LEG DOES NOT SHOW. It reads cargo-binstall's own source. It says nothing about
# what Cargo itself verifies when the compile fallback runs `cargo install --locked`, which
# is a different program; that remains unproven item 11(b).
#
